 talk you know i think going into that  meeting  everybody was really gung-ho about  getting back with our players and  and understanding you know that there  are implications for code and everybody  knew it but i think  um after our meeting with the medical  people there were some  eye-opening things there at least for  myself that i was unaware of at the time  so  what stood out can you give me one of  those bullet points  yeah you know i think the increased  concern over the cardiovascular thing  when you look at eduardo rodriguez the  pitcher for the red sox who's out with  an inflamed heart  um the young man in indiana you know  feeney the offensive lineman  that had an incident they brought up  there was a pitcher in um  the tacoma area in washington a high  school kid uh 18 year old kid that died  um from covet in the springtime um and  that could have been part of it also um  so some of those things i think  there was also a basketball player that  played a florida state that was playing  overseas that passed away  um so they came you know that starts to  hit home a little bit when you're  starting to hear about  you know it's a little bit and the other  thing that we were made aware of is that  you could be asymptomatic and still have  heart issues and some of these heart  issues wouldn't be  um detected unless you had a cardiac mri  and those are a little bit different and  hard to get from a testing standpoint is  the way they explain it to us  how did you tell your team we had a zoom  meeting you know we're still not allowed  to meet with them with that many people  in person  so uh we got the news of one and then we  got everybody on a zoom call  at 1 30 and then our position coaches  called everybody individuals and we're  in the process of touching base with all  of our kids individually and then i was  down at workouts  um yesterday i'll be back down again  today to talk to him face to face  or mask the mask i should say yeah i'm  trying to understand this chip where  the information you have with the pac-12  how  is that different is it different than  what the acc or the sec or the big 12  have or the big 10 has  i would love these medical communities  to get together  and and maybe make it on a zoom call and  share their information like we should  understand this  i think to be fair to the sport student  athlete and really  to society what what is at stake here  and what are we finding out and why  aren't we cross-pollinating and sharing  that information  that's a great point you know i i don't  know how that community talks  um the other issue is that the  prevalence of cases  in the pac-12 communities is different  than the prevalence of cases in  nebraska so to speak so i think there's  there's also  there can't be a one-size-fit-all from  as i understand this  because it some areas have been infected  a lot more and other other areas aren't  as infected  you know so um i think that has to be  taken into consideration  there are different parts of the country  that aren't as infected as this and  that's  that's the deal and then the next step  becomes testing so  if you have a community that has a lot  of covert cases then your  testing is slower than places when  there's not a lot of  a lot of it that's why i don't think  there's a one size fit all  for some of these things and that's  where it gets frustrating when you look  at it overall and say  how come and we have to answer those  questions how come they can practice and  we can't practice  but there were times earlier when we  would send tests away but it would be  except take seven to ten days  um to get test results back and  understanding that is that  we're trying to get our asymptomatic  third string left guard tested  and there's people on ventilators that  need to get tested so there's a  you know there's a social uh  responsibility that goes along with it  too  when you're in a community that has a  lot of coving cases  has it hit you yet that you're not going  to be coaching football this fall  a little bit um but obviously it's not  all us i mean  women's soccer can't play you know i go  back to the spring  when we were sitting in a meeting in in  our basketball coaches you know corey  closing our women's basketball team was  getting ready to be a high ranked team  in the  ncaa tournament so they were affected  our baseball and softball teams were  affected in the springtime  um so it is football and that's our job  and that's our lives and that's what we  care about our players but  everybody's been effective at this um  mikey ann  who's a great sports media guy out here  in the pac-12 i just saw that he just  got released and uh  is not working because the pac-12  network isn't going to be broadcasting  games in the fall so  it's a little bit bigger and i think we  have to step back as individuals and say  poor me it's  it's there's a lot of people that have  have had uh  implications from this and and we  somehow have to get a handle on it  because it's  it's not just sports related it's  everything related and  um when you get a death toll over 160  000 in this country  you know sometimes when you say hey i  can't play football i  maybe there's some other things that are  a little bit more important than us  playing football right now  i know you hope to play football in the  spring do you expect  to play football in the spring you know  what that's a great  question then and i don't know if we can  um and i've said that you know can you  play two seasons  in in one calendar year and what is that  told um  our whole goal and everything we do in  our chancellor said it our north star  that guides us is the health and safety  of our players so  you know we better really look as from a  health and safety standpoint  say we have a vaccine can you actually  play a football season in the spring and  then come back and play a football  season in the fall  that's never been done but i think we  need to look into that part too  before we just say hey automatically  we're just going to play in the  springtime because  you know how safe is that and i don't  know that information and  when we get to situations where it's  medical and some of those things  i'm going to defer to the experts in  terms of what they they will tell us  because i don't i don't have the answers  in that situation  thank you coach um do we know if players  are going to be able to transfer  are you uh concerned if any of your  players would be able to go  and try to play some place this this  fall  yeah we haven't heard anything from the  ncaa they just made a ruling yesterday  about  eligibility you know you would retain  your eligibility if you opted out  or if you played in less than 50 of the  games um so that's the first ruling that  we've gotten i think  this is all new for everybody you know  as it came down with both the big ten  and the pac-12 and then you know i know  obviously we're the power five  conferences but you feel for the guys in  the mountain west and the mac  you know they they're done and then  people have already forgotten about the  ivy league and division two and division  three and  you know we're not the only ones that  have this but um the ncaa has  has to really come out quickly here with  a with some rulings for everybody  because um players have to make  decisions i don't know exactly what that  ruling is and  would kids be instantly eligible or not  instantly eligible  and um that's there's a lot of lot of  work to be done even though we're not  playing football but there's a lot of  work to be done especially  administratively  how about usc and ucla just have a  scrimmage in november  and we televise it you okay with that i  would love that and i think clay would  love that too  we we could put a bubble over the rose  bowl and  you could live up at the rose bowl for  uh  for a couple weeks so that we made sure  there was no covet and then  and then uh and then go play i think  that would be a great experience for  everybody  that'd be great uh coach good luck and  uh thanks for joining us thanks dan  appreciate it  that's uh chip kelly ucla head coach

